  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 28f310e05cc019a48855581da30b411e, type: 3}
  m_Name: Adobo
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  lines:
  - text: ' Adobo is one of the oldest dishes in Philippine cuisine,'
    characterSprite: {fileID: -511706578061346315, guid: ae71825725b21a3459805439433622ee, type: 3}
  - text: With roots that reach back before the arrival of the Spanish colonizers
    characterSprite: {fileID: -511706578061346315, guid: ae71825725b21a3459805439433622ee, type: 3}
  - text: With roots that reach back before the arrival of the Spanish colonizers
    characterSprite: {fileID: -511706578061346315, guid: ae71825725b21a3459805439433622ee, type: 3}
  - text: The name comes from the Spanish word "adobar,"
    characterSprite: {fileID: -511706578061346315, guid: ae71825725b21a3459805439433622ee, type: 3}
  - text: Meaning to marinate or season, but Filipinos were already cooking meat
      in vinegar long before that name arrived
    characterSprite: {fileID: -511706578061346315, guid: ae71825725b21a3459805439433622ee, type: 3}
  - text: Filipino cooks have passed down recipes like Adobo through generations,
    characterSprite: {fileID: -511706578061346315, guid: ae71825725b21a3459805439433622ee, type: 3}
  - text: Adjusting vinegar, soy sauce, and garlic to their family's taste
    characterSprite: {fileID: -511706578061346315, guid: ae71825725b21a3459805439433622ee, type: 3}
